-- ============================================================================
-- PD2 EXPERT DEEP SEED: Gas Lift Design, advanced tier.
--
-- Six modules, 26 lessons, 132 questions (15 per module bank plus a 42
-- question final exam). The structure row is GENERATED from the tier's
-- manifest.json rather than hand typed.
--
-- What this tier owns: THE UNLOADING SEQUENCE AND THE POINT OF INJECTION. What
-- happens when the design is actually run, and how deep the gas really gets.
--
-- THE MECHANISM OF THE KNIFE EDGE, STATED OUTRIGHT. Across every charged valve
-- the open flag IS the test of the casing DROP since a valve's own opening
-- stage against that valve's SPREAD. On the published midDecrementKnifeEdge
-- case, spaced on 26.75 psi per valve, valve 4 opens with a spread of
-- 32.272254090 psi against a drop of 32.122462454 psi, and the 0.149791635 psi
-- between them is the whole verdict. It multipoints at stages 2 to 5 and is
-- clean at 1, 6 and 7, and a quarter of a psi of decrement flips it.
--
-- The trap that follows: the SURFACE decrement is 26.75 psi per valve while
-- the drop a valve actually FEELS at depth runs from 28.652797457 to
-- 33.355029522 psi. Compare a spread against the decrement rather than against
-- the drop and you pick the wrong valves.
--
-- THE LESSON THE WHOLE PROGRAMME HAS BEEN BUILDING TOWARD. Before engines PR
-- #110, `oracle_gaslift.py`'s `unloading()` walked the valves and appended an
-- empty open-valve list for every stage WITHOUT EVER EVALUATING THE CONDITION,
-- and no `unloading` key was written into the goldens at all. So the single
-- most consequential boolean `designGasLift` emits, the one that says the
-- string will inject at two depths at once, had NO independent check while the
-- file still looked like coverage. The engine's own gate asserted only a
-- direction, one decrement far below every spread and one far above, both
-- outside the design band, so it tested the direction of the rule and not the
-- rule.
--
-- COVERAGE THAT IS NOT COVERAGE IS WORSE THAN NO COVERAGE, because no coverage
-- is visible and false coverage is not. #110 closed it by deriving the verdict
-- from the published closing rule at valve depth off a forward RK4 column,
-- where the engine evaluates it at surface by inverting a coarser column, so
-- the two routes are genuinely independent, and it found a production operated
-- disagreement on its first run.
--
-- A CHORD THAT CANNOT SEE ITSELF, AND THE FINDING IS THE RATIO. Both sides of
-- `deepestInjectionPoint`'s residual come off the same pair of chords, so the
-- residual it reports is a statement that the two chords agree with each other
-- and nothing more. On the published case the shipped engine reports 4.6770e-3
-- psi against a true 1.58211e-1, 33.83 times larger, with the depth
-- 1.317711139 ft off. On the wave's teaching traverse the same mechanism gives
-- 60.420814470 ft of error, 1.5907e-2 psi reported against 1.0789e+1 true, a
-- ratio of 678.26 that holds near 670 down the whole refinement. The published
-- case alone makes the defect look negligible.
--
-- Worse, the reported residual is ANTI-CORRELATED with accuracy: across a
-- column refinement it RISES from exactly 0.0000e+0 psi at 8 samples to
-- 4.8890e-3 at 2560 while the depth improves, so the 8 sample row reports a
-- perfect residual and carries the worst depth in the study. An engineer
-- tightening their acceptance tolerance on that number selects a worse answer.
--
-- The capstone carries the same mechanism at its own size: a 26.52 ft gap
-- between the injection depth read off the shipped tabulation and the one read
-- off a refined one, on a well where that depth is what the whole design was
-- bought for.
-- ============================================================================

